# OWNER-LOGIN DEPLOYMENT — Compose INTERPOLATION values for docker-compose.image.yml and its overrides
# (docker compose --env-file <this file>). The ONLY secrets here are the database values below (DB-SETUP, like Twenty:
# compose builds the bundled db and its admin URL from PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD; no container ever receives PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD,
# and only the one-shot db-setup service receives PG_ADMIN_URL or PG_DATABASE_URL, the Twenty Helm model);
# otherwise only image references, host paths, the API bind address/port and resource limits. Copy to e.g.
# /etc/crewflow/compose.env (root:root 0600). Passing --env-file also stops Compose from reading a stray .env next to the
# compose files.
# Lint it: node packages/rab-docker/scripts/compose-owner-login.cjs --lint-env-file compose /etc/crewflow/compose.env
#
# DATABASE (DB-SETUP, owner decisions D1 a / D4 / D5; the Twenty Helm model, OWNER-DB-FINAL.md Part A; PG_ADMIN_URL, owner
# decision 2026-11-17: the admin login lives ONLY in the one-shot db-setup step, and PG_DATABASE_URL ALWAYS means the app
# login, crewflow_owner). Choose ONE of three modes; only the one-shot db-setup service receives these values, rab-api and
# rab-worker never do (they build the crewflow_owner URL from db-setup's credential-free target file and its crewflow_owner
# password file, and refuse PG_ADMIN_URL). No psql, no manual step.
#
# (a) BUNDLED db (the default; CREWFLOW_BUNDLED_DB unset or 1). REQUIRED in this mode only: the bundled db's superuser
#     password, 32+ URL-safe characters (generate it: openssl rand -hex 32). It is read only on the db volume's first start;
#     changing it later needs ALTER ROLE postgres in the db container first. db-setup creates crewflow_owner with a RANDOM
#     password it keeps on its own volume and reuses on every start (the Twenty Helm model; APP_SECRET is never part of it).
#     A missing value is refused at start (db, db-setup) and by the lint.
PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD=<openssl-rand-hex-32>
#
# YOUR OWN database: CREWFLOW_BUNDLED_DB=0 (like Twenty's Helm db.enabled=false: `db` gets 0 replicas and never starts),
# REMOVE PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD above (not needed, and refused by the lint: no dummy value), and set exactly ONE of the two
# URLs below, at the DIRECT host (never a transaction pooler: Neon -pooler, Supabase :6543, DigitalOcean :25061, RDS Proxy,
# PgBouncer :6432), sslmode=verify-full, a host other than `db`:
# CREWFLOW_BUNDLED_DB=0
# (b) PG_ADMIN_URL: your database's ADMIN URL. The login must be able to create roles (CREATEROLE or superuser) and to
#     grant on the database; db-setup creates crewflow_owner if it is missing, with a random password stored on its volume
#     and reused. Never the crewflow_owner login (put that in PG_DATABASE_URL).
# PG_ADMIN_URL=postgresql://<admin-login>:<password>@<db-direct-host>:5432/<db-name>?sslmode=verify-full
# (c) PG_DATABASE_URL: the crewflow_owner URL you created yourself (postgres-init/owner-login/01-crewflow-owner.sql;
#     DB-SETUP-PLAN §7.1), and NO PG_ADMIN_URL. db-setup does no provisioning: it only checks it, read-only (never
#     SUPERUSER / BYPASSRLS, PostgreSQL 16+), and uses it UNCHANGED (the database is never written to, the password never
#     changed). Its login MUST be crewflow_owner: any other login is refused (before 2026-11-17 this variable held the admin
#     URL; that meaning was renamed to PG_ADMIN_URL and is never silently reused). PG_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL is refused too.
# PG_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://crewflow_owner:<password>@<db-direct-host>:5432/<db-name>?sslmode=verify-full
#
# Image: the approved release's multi-platform INDEX digest (amd64 + arm64). The variable is named like Twenty's TAG but
# accepts ONLY a digest (sha256:<64 hex>); a plain tag is refused. ONE image, ONE variable runs the API (which migrates on
# start, O3), the Worker and the owner CLI one-off commands run from rab-api (O1/O2, X2.2; published under the one tag
# <full commit>; take the digest from the release manifest). Deploy: `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`.
# Rollback: put the previous digest here, same command.
# Refused here: CREWFLOW_IMAGE_DIGEST (this variable's name until 2026-11-11) and any per-role variable
# (CREWFLOW_API_IMAGE_* / CREWFLOW_WORKER_IMAGE_*): there is no API-only or Worker-only deploy.
CREWFLOW_IMAGE_TAG=sha256:<image-index-digest-64-hex>
# Only to override the release registry (default adhin007/adolphus):
# CREWFLOW_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=<registry/repository>
#
# Migration approval (BC-11, C4): unset = none, the API applies pending migrations on start without a token (like
# Twenty). token = opt-in safety net: an API start that would apply to a non-fresh database needs
# CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVED=<token> for exactly the pending set (passed to that one start only, never in a file). Set it
# HERE only: the lint refuses it in database.env / runtime.env, where rab-api's committed value would override it.
# CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVAL_MODE=token
# Redis 7.x, pinned by digest; record the tag it was resolved from.
CREWFLOW_REDIS_IMAGE=redis@sha256:<redis-7-alpine-index-digest-64-hex>
# Only to override the bundled db's committed image (postgres:16, Debian, by digest): another Debian postgres:16, :17 or :18 digest (never alpine: the volume layout is the Debian one).
# CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres@sha256:<postgres-index-digest-64-hex>
#
# Host paths (absolute). The secret directory (root 0700) holds database.env, runtime.env and redis-users.acl.
CREWFLOW_SECRETS_DIR=/etc/crewflow/secrets
# EXTERNAL database only, when its CA is not in the system store: the provider's CA bundle (mounted at
# /run/crewflow/pg-ca.crt; set PG_SSL_CA_CERT_PATH to that path in database.env). The bundled db needs none. Without a file
# to mount, put the PEM text in database.env as PG_SSL_CA_CERT instead (and leave this unset).
# CREWFLOW_PG_CA_FILE=/etc/crewflow/pg-ca.crt
#
# Release 0.7.0 (HWS cutover, owner rule: no cutover without a verified backup): the HOST directory holding the pre-cutover
# pg_dump (custom format) and its marker.json, mounted read-only into rab-api at /run/crewflow/hws-cutover-backup. Needed for
# the first 0.7.0 start only (runbook: docs/release/MIGRATION-AND-ROLLBACK-PLAN.md §4.7); unset = no backup, and a start with
# anything to copy refuses (nothing copied, the affected workspaces answer 503 until a start with a verified backup).
# CREWFLOW_HWS_CUTOVER_BACKUP_DIR=/srv/crewflow/hws-cutover-backup
#
# The address and host port the edge proxy connects to. Never 0.0.0.0. Pick a free host port when another service already
# uses 3000 on this host (Oracle VM: OWNER-LOGIN-ORACLE-VM-PLAN.md).
CREWFLOW_API_BIND=127.0.0.1
CREWFLOW_API_HOST_PORT=3000
#
# Resource limits (cpus must not exceed the host's vCPU count; Docker refuses to start the container otherwise).
CREWFLOW_API_MEM_LIMIT=1024m
CREWFLOW_API_CPUS=2
CREWFLOW_WORKER_MEM_LIMIT=1536m
CREWFLOW_WORKER_CPUS=2
CREWFLOW_REDIS_MAXMEMORY=384mb
CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT=1024m
CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_CPUS=2
#
# MUST NOT BE SET here: any other password, URL, token (CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVED included) or secret, and any
# application variable (NODE_ENV, other PG_*, REDIS_URL, APP_SECRET, CREWFLOW_DB_MODE, ...). COMPOSE_FILE / COMPOSE_PROFILES /
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: name the files explicitly with -f instead.
